A||N||9||strick8||Engine swap and A/C||||1||
Z||000000||strick8||06-08-2000||09:43 AM||||Getting ready to pull my 2.5 4L from a 87 coupe and put in a 2.8 V6 from a 87GT.  Can I use the A/C compressor form the 4L?  Someone told me I had to use a V6 compressor.  Are they not the same compressor?||||reg||1
Z||000001||redfiero||06-08-2000||11:30 AM||||I believe the compressor from an 87 4 cyl is the same as an 88 4 cyl.  Also, a compressor from an 87 6 cyl is the same as an 86 6 cyl.  The two however are different!||||reg||1
Z||000002||redfiero||06-08-2000||11:31 AM||||I believe the compressor from an 87 4 cyl is the same as an 88 4 cyl.  Also, a compressor from an 87 6 cyl is the same as an 86 6 cyl.  The two however are different!||||reg||1
Z||000003||strick8||06-08-2000||07:22 PM||||Well if it is a different compressor, will the rest of my A/C system work with a V6 compressor?||||reg||1
Z||000004||dman||06-09-2000||02:16 PM||||Hi strick8. I'm sorry, I don't have an answer to your question. I too was interested in finding out the answer to your question. Sometimes though posts can get buried and then forgotten, maybe my "reply" will help bring your query back to the top and someone will reply.<P>There are at least two different compressors available. Does anyone know if they are interchangeable?<P>Thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||06-09-2000||03:27 PM||||The 87 2.5 engines used a serpentine belt where as all the Fiero V6 engines used V-belts. That's one difference I know about for sure. I suspect the brackets are completely different too. Whether you can physically mount the 2.5 compressor onto the 2.8, I don't know. I bet with a little modification and changing the pulley, it would work though. I can't think of any real reason why the rest of the A/C system would be any different.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||06-09-2000||05:46 PM||||The compressors are interchangable.  I checked on the fierostore website, and the compressor is the same for both motors...||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||06-10-2000||03:30 PM||||87-88 4 cyl fiero uses a V5 compressor that IS NOT the same as either the V6 or older 4 cyl ones.<P>There is more to it than just the pully. The compressor itself is a totaly diferant design. Some of electrical controls are diferant as well, but I don't remember just what. (Pressure switch locations are diferant I think. V5 has all the switches on the compressor. A6 only has one of the switches on it. The other switch on an A6 system is on the ACC/Dryer.)<P>The least painless swap, I think, would be to find a V5 that has the V belt pully & clutch. This would work with no other changes to the system. You might have to make a bracket for the back of the compressor, and/or alter the clutch end brackets, but metal is eaier for most people to work up than hoses and wiring.<P>One of the advantages of the V5 is that it is varaible displacement. It doesn't present the load to the engine that the old A6 does. It will reduce it's load to match the AC system demand at the time. A6 always runs full load no mater what the AC system needs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||strick8||06-12-2000||10:34 AM||||If I can change the pulley and get the compressor to fit on the V6 bracket, will the computer let it work?  The V5 compressor receivers a single that tells it to engage or disengage right?  Because the A6 runs at full load all the time will it need a different single from the computer, if any at all?||||reg||5
Z||000009||theogre||06-12-2000||08:09 PM||||The ECM line simply turns the clutch on or off. Or does it monitor that the clutch is on or off? I can't look it up right now. (I think it monitors the line to bump the idle up when the clutch is engergized.) That should be the same on the other ECM's I think. <P>The compressor displacement is controled internally by the compressor. The ECM doesn't know anything about it.||||reg||1
